\section{Fixed sets of literal values}{

Quoted options (\code{"basis" | "bundle" | ...}) are the literal strings (or,
occasionally, \code{TRUE}/\code{FALSE}/\code{NULL} mixed into the same set) mosaic
actually recognizes there -- pass exactly one of them, quoted, e.g.
\code{curve = "basis"}. A set with more than 10 values is abbreviated to the
first 10 plus \code{...}; see the property's own description (same line) or
\href{https://idl.uw.edu/mosaic/}{mosaic's documentation} for the rest --
most commonly \href{https://observablehq.com/plot/features/scales#color-scales}{Observable Plot's color schemes} for
a \verb{scheme =} argument.

One special literal, \code{"Fixed"}, appears on domain-like arguments
(\verb{x_domain =}, \verb{color_domain =}, ...): it freezes a domain that was
initially computed from the data so it no longer changes under
subsequent interactive filtering, for stable before/after comparisons.

Two more appear together, \code{"day"/"week"/"month"/...} and \code{number}, on
interval-like arguments (\verb{interval =}, \verb{ticks =}, ...): either a named
time interval (\code{"day"}, \code{"week"}, \code{"3 months"}, ...) or a plain number,
defining intervals at that many underlying units.
